{"name":"Christmas Gold","hex":"#caa906","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"gold","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A heavy yellow elemental metal of great value, with atomic number 79.","A coin or coinage made of this material, or supposedly so."]},{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["☉ (alchemy)"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/gold"}],"entries":[{"title":"U&Gold","blocks":[{"text":"U&Gold is a British premium television channel from the UKTV network that was launched in November 1992 as UK Gold before it was rebranded UKTV Gold in 2004. In 2008, it was split into flagship channel Gold and miscellaneous channel Watch, with classic comedy based programming airing on Gold, non-crime drama and entertainment programming airing on Watch, and quiz shows and more high-brow comedy airing on Dave. In 2024, Gold was rebranded as U&Gold. It shows repeats of classic programming from the BBC, ITV and other broadcasters. Every December, from 2015 until 2018, the channel was temporarily renamed Christmas Gold. This has since been discontinued, though the channel continues to broadcast Christmas comedy."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/U%26Gold"},{"title":"Christmas","blocks":[{"text":"Christmas is a festival commemorating the birth of Jesus Christ, observed primarily on December 25 as a religious and cultural celebration among billions of people around the world. A liturgical feast central to Christianity, Christmas preparation begins on the First Sunday of Advent. Advent is followed by Christmastide, which historically in the West lasts twelve days and culminates on Twelfth Night. Christmas Day is a public holiday in many countries and is observed by a majority of Christians; it is also celebrated culturally by many non-Christians and forms an integral part of the annual festive holiday season."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/NativityChristmasLights2.jpg/330px-NativityChristmasLights2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Christmas"},{"title":"Gold","blocks":[{"text":"Gold is a chemical element; its chemical symbol is Au and atomic number 79. In its pure form, it is a bright-metallic-yellow, dense, soft, malleable, and ductile metal. Chemically, gold is a transition metal, a group 11 element, and one of the noble metals. It is one of the least reactive chemical elements, being the second lowest in the reactivity series, with only platinum ranked as less reactive. Gold is solid under standard conditions."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Gold-crystals.jpg/330px-Gold-crystals.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold"},{"title":"Gold (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Gold, also called golden, is a color tone resembling the chemical element gold."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg/330px-GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold_(color)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/UKTV_Gold_share_of_viewing.png/500px-UKTV_Gold_share_of_viewing.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/UKTV_Gold_share_of_viewing.png/500px-UKTV_Gold_share_of_viewing.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"UKTV Gold share of viewing BARB figures 1992–2007","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AUKTV_Gold_share_of_viewing.png"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/2/24/UK_Gold_Goldie_1993.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/2/24/UK_Gold_Goldie_1993.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"'Goldie' the UK Gold dog, used from 1992 to 1993","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AUK_Gold_Goldie_1993.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/4/48/UK_Gold_Keeping_Up_Appearances_holding_slide.jpg/330px-UK_Gold_Keeping_Up_Appearances_holding_slide.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/4/48/UK_Gold_Keeping_Up_Appearances_holding_slide.jpg/330px-UK_Gold_Keeping_Up_Appearances_holding_slide.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A holding slide for the television programme Keeping Up Appearances which demonstrates the 1997–1999 corporate style","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AUK_Gold_Keeping_Up_Appearances_holding_slide.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f9/Adoration_of_the_sheperds_-_Matthias_Stomer.jpg/500px-Adoration_of_the_sheperds_-_Matthias_Stomer.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f9/Adoration_of_the_sheperds_-_Matthias_Stomer.jpg/1280px-Adoration_of_the_sheperds_-_Matthias_Stomer.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f9/Adoration_of_the_sheperds_-_Matthias_Stomer.jpg/500px-Adoration_of_the_sheperds_-_Matthias_Stomer.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Adoration of the Shepherds by Dutch painter Matthias Stomer, 1632","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAdoration_of_the_sheperds_-_Matthias_Stomer.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Hortus_Deliciarum%2C_Die_Geburt_Christi.JPG/500px-Hortus_Deliciarum%2C_Die_Geburt_Christi.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thum